# Whistleblower Protection Policy

**{{ org_name }}** -- a 501(c)(3) nonprofit research institute incorporated in {{ state }} (EIN: {{ ein }})

## 1. Purpose

{{ org_name }} ("the Organization") is committed to lawful and ethical conduct in all of its activities. This policy is intended to encourage and enable individuals to raise serious concerns about suspected misconduct, fraud, or violations of law within the Organization rather than overlooking a problem or seeking resolution outside the Organization. The Organization will not tolerate retaliation against anyone who, in good faith, reports a suspected violation.

This policy is adopted in compliance with IRS Form 990 Part VI Line 13 requirements and the whistleblower protections codified in the Sarbanes-Oxley Act of 2002, Section 1107 (18 U.S.C. Section 1513(e)), which apply to all entities including nonprofits.

## 2. Scope

This policy applies to all directors, officers, employees, volunteers, and contractors of the Organization (collectively, "Covered Persons").

## 3. Reportable Conduct

A Covered Person should report any activity they reasonably believe involves:

- Fraud, theft, embezzlement, or financial irregularities
- Violations of federal, state, or local law
- Violations of the Organization's policies, bylaws, or code of conduct
- Misuse of the Organization's assets or funds
- Falsification of financial records, tax filings, or grant reports
- Retaliation against a person who has made a good-faith report under this policy
- Any other conduct that poses a substantial and specific danger to public health or safety

## 4. Reporting Procedures

A Covered Person who becomes aware of reportable conduct should report the matter as soon as practicable using one of the following channels:

1. **Board Chair**: Contact the Board Chair directly in writing (email or letter).
2. **Any Board Member**: If the concern involves the Board Chair, or if the reporter is more comfortable contacting another director, the report may be directed to any current member of the Board of Directors.
3. **Written submission**: Reports may also be submitted in writing and delivered to any board member by mail, email, or other written medium.

Reports should include sufficient detail to allow for an adequate investigation, including a description of the conduct, the individuals involved, relevant dates, and any supporting documentation. Anonymous reports will be accepted and investigated to the extent possible, though anonymity may limit the Organization's ability to fully investigate.

## 5. Protection from Retaliation

No Covered Person who in good faith reports suspected misconduct shall be subject to retaliation, harassment, demotion, suspension, termination, threats, or any other adverse action as a result of making such a report.

Any individual who retaliates against a whistleblower will be subject to disciplinary action up to and including termination of employment or removal from the Board of Directors.

Protection under this policy extends to any person who participates in an investigation of a reported concern, including witnesses and those who provide information.

This policy does not protect individuals who file reports they know to be false or misleading, or who themselves participated in the reported misconduct.

## 6. Investigation Procedures

Upon receiving a report, the Board Chair (or designated board member if the Chair is the subject of the report) shall:

1. Acknowledge receipt of the report to the reporter within five (5) business days, unless the report was made anonymously.
2. Determine whether the report warrants a formal investigation.
3. Conduct or oversee the investigation promptly and impartially. The Board may retain outside counsel or other advisors as needed.
4. Report findings and any recommended corrective action to the full Board of Directors.
5. Communicate the outcome to the reporter to the extent appropriate and permitted by law.

All Covered Persons are expected to cooperate fully with any investigation under this policy.

## 7. Confidentiality

The Organization will treat all reports and related investigations as confidential to the fullest extent possible, consistent with the need to conduct an adequate investigation and comply with applicable law. The identity of the reporter will not be disclosed without their consent unless disclosure is required by law or is necessary for the investigation.

## 8. Record Keeping

The Organization shall retain the following records related to this policy:

- All written reports of suspected violations
- Documentation of investigations, findings, and corrective actions taken
- Records of any disciplinary actions resulting from investigations

These records shall be maintained in a secure and confidential manner for a minimum of seven (7) years, consistent with the Organization's document retention policy and applicable legal requirements. It is a violation of federal law (18 U.S.C. Section 1519) to destroy, alter, or conceal documents with the intent to obstruct a federal investigation.

## 9. Distribution and Acknowledgment

This policy shall be distributed to all Covered Persons and made available to new directors, officers, employees, volunteers, and contractors upon the commencement of their relationship with the Organization.

## 10. Amendments

This policy may be amended by a resolution of the Board of Directors at any duly convened meeting at which a quorum is present.

